## 🧩 The Problem

Government departments run independent portals, registries, and legacy systems that were never built to talk to each other. The result:

- Citizens repeatedly submit the **same documents** across multiple offices
- No **unified way to track** an application across departments
- Officials lack a **consolidated view** of applications, approvals, and outcomes
- Sharing citizen data between departments means moving sensitive information around — a real security and privacy risk that makes departments hesitant to integrate in the first place

## 💡 Our Solution

Every existing approach to this problem (API marketplaces, federated identity, data-sharing pipelines) is built on one assumption: **data has to move for departments to trust each other.**

**Pramaan flips that.** Departments verify facts about a citizen — *"is this person's income below ₹2.5 lakh?"* — using cryptographic proofs, without ever seeing the citizen's actual document or data. The document stays exactly where it was issued. Only a mathematically verifiable **proof** travels.

## ✨ Key Features

- 🔐 **Zero-knowledge proof verification** — prove eligibility without revealing the underlying data
- 🆔 **Single Application Number (SAN)** — one tracking number across every department involved
- 🤝 **AI-assisted schema mapping** — auto-translates data formats between mismatched department systems
- 🔌 **Non-invasive legacy connectors** — onboard old systems via change-data-capture, zero code changes required
- ✅ **Scoped, revocable consent** — citizens control exactly what's shared, with whom, and for how long
- ⛓️ **Tamper-evident audit log** — real SHA-256 hash-chained logging, independently verifiable
- 📊 **SLA monitoring dashboard** — predictive alerts for officials before applications breach deadlines
- 🔔 **Proactive eligibility notifications** — the system tells citizens what they qualify for, instead of making them search

## 🔍 How It's Different

| Existing Systems | Pramaan |
|---|---|
| Move the actual document/data | Moves a cryptographic proof, never the raw data |
| Require departments to rebuild/standardize | AI-assisted mapping connects mismatched legacy systems as-is |
| Centralize or duplicate citizen data | Data stays decentralized at its source department |
| Manual audit trails | Real-time, tamper-evident hash-chained logging |

## 🏗️ Architecture

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    C[Citizen App] -->|Generate Proof| PM[Pramaan Mesh]
    PM -->|Verified Proof Only| D1[Department A]
    PM -->|Verified Proof Only| D2[Department B]
    D1 -.->|Raw data stays here| DB1[(Dept A Database)]
    D2 -.->|Raw data stays here| DB2[(Dept B Database)]
    PM --> AL[(Tamper-Evident Audit Log)]
    PM --> AI[AI Schema Mapping Engine]
    D1 --> CDC1[CDC Connector]
    D2 --> CDC2[CDC Connector]
```

See [`ARCHITECTURE.md`](./ARCHITECTURE.md) for the full system breakdown.

## 🛠️ Tech Stack

| Layer | Technology |
|---|---|
| Frontend | Next.js (React), TypeScript, Tailwind CSS, shadcn/ui |
| Backend | Node.js (NestJS / Firebase Cloud Functions) |
| Database | PostgreSQL (Prisma ORM) / Firestore |
| Auth | Firebase Authentication (OTP + Email), OAuth2/OIDC |
| Real-time | WebSockets / Firestore listeners |
| Crypto | zk-SNARKs (snarkjs + circom), SHA-256 hash chaining |
| AI | Gemini API (schema mapping) |
| Infra | Docker, GitHub Actions CI/CD, Prometheus + Grafana, Sentry |

## 🚀 Getting Started

### Prerequisites
- Node.js 18+
- npm or pnpm
- A Firebase project (or PostgreSQL instance, depending on backend variant)

### Installation

```bash
# Clone the repository
git clone https://github.com/<your-username>/pramaan.git
cd pramaan

# Install dependencies
npm install

# Set up environment variables
cp .env.example .env.local
# fill in the required values — see Environment Variables below

# Run database migrations (if using Prisma/Postgres)
npx prisma migrate dev

# Start the development server
npm run dev
```

The app should now be running at `http://localhost:3000`.

## 🔑 Environment Variables

Create a `.env.local` file with the following:

```env
# Database
DATABASE_URL=

# Auth
NEXTAUTH_SECRET=
FIREBASE_API_KEY=
FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID=

# AI
GEMINI_API_KEY=

# Misc
NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL=http://localhost:3000
```

> ⚠️ Never commit `.env.local` — it's already included in `.gitignore`.

